ortant about the photoelectric effect is what Albert Einstein deduced from it. Einstein realized that there were several characteristics of the photoelectric effect that could be explained only if <em data-effect="italics">EM radiation is itself quantized</em>: the apparently continuous stream of energy in an EM wave is actually composed of energy quanta called photons. In his explanation of the photoelectric effect, Einstein defined a quantized unit or quantum of EM energy, which we now call a <span data-type="term" id="import-auto-id1575989" group-by="p">photon</span>, with an energy proportional to the frequency of EM radiation. In equation form, the <span data-type="term" id="import-auto-id1477392" group-by="p">photon energy</span> is</p><div data-type="equation" id="eip-588"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{E =  ital "hf"} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">29.4</span>
</div>
</div><p id="import-auto-id1094249">where <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{E} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the energy of a photon of frequency <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{f} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> and <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{h} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is Planck’s constant. This revolutionary idea looks similar to Planck’s quantization of energy states in blackbody oscillators, but it is quite different. It is the quantization of EM radiation itself. EM waves are composed of photons and are not continuous smooth waves as described in previous chapters on optics. Their energy is absorbed and emitted in lumps, not continuously. This is exactly consistent with Planck’s quantization of energy levels in blackbody oscillators, since these oscillators increase and decrease their energy in steps of <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{ ital "hf"} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> by absorbing and emitting photons having <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{E =  ital "hf"} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>. We do not observe this with our eyes, because there are so many photons in common light sources that individual photons go unnoticed. (See <a href="29-2-the-photoelectric-effect#import-auto-id1104097" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 29.8">Figure 29.8</a>.) The next section of the text (<a href="29-3-photon-energies-and-the-electromagnetic-spectrum" aria-label="Go to Photon Energies and the Electromagnetic Spectrum" data-page-slug="29-3-photon-energies-and-the-electromagnetic-spectrum" data-page-uuid="3a247428-8d85-4d9d-9494-d8a75bc7bd66" data-page-fragment="">Photon Energies and the Electromagnetic Spectrum</a>) is devoted to a discussion of photons and some of their characteristics and implications. For now, we will use the photon concept to explain the photoelectric effect, much as Einstein did.</p><div class="os-figure" id="import-auto-id1104097"><figure data-id="import-auto-id1104097" class="ui-has-child-figcaption"><span data-type="media" id="import-auto-id1434446" data-alt="Light rays coming out of a flashlight. The photons are depicted as small ellipses enclosing a wave each and moving in the direction of the rays. Energies of photons are labeled as E and E prime, where E is equal to h f and E prime is equal to h f prime.">
        <button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of Light rays coming out of a flashlight. The photons are depicted as small ellipses enclosing a wave each and moving in the direction of the rays. Energies of photons are labeled as E and E prime, where E is equal to h f and E prime is equal to h f prime." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/3d78ea95dbec987c88c0d5682f237c831781a129" data-media-type="image/jpg" alt="Light rays coming out of a flashlight. The photons are depicted as small ellipses enclosing a wave each and moving in the direction of the rays. Energies of photons are labeled as E and E prime, where E is equal to h f and E prime is equal to h f prime." width="876" height="230" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/3d78ea95dbec987c88c0d5682f237c831781a129"></button>
      </span>

      <figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">29.8</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">An EM wave of frequency <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{f} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math> is composed of photons, or individual quanta of EM radiation. The energy of each photon is <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>hf</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{E =  ital "hf"} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math>, where <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{h} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math> is Planck’s constant and <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{f} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math> i